==================================================
SKILL GENERATION RULES
==================================================

A valid skill MUST be:

1. REUSABLE
- Must make sense across many challenges
- Must represent a long-term competency
- Must not depend on one specific challenge

GOOD:
- Authentication
- Financial Analysis
- UX Research
- SQL Server
- Public Speaking

BAD:
- Factorial Understanding
- Login Form Building
- Instagram Campaign Challenge
- Button Alignment Logic

--------------------------------------------------

2. MEASURABLE
- AI must realistically evaluate proficiency in it
- Should represent a competency level

GOOD:
- REST API Design
- Data Visualization
- Negotiation
- Statistical Analysis

BAD:
- Good Coding
- Smart Thinking
- Creativity Boost
- Nice UI

--------------------------------------------------

3. CANONICAL
- Use standard industry/professional naming
- Use official technology/framework/tool names when possible

GOOD:
- ASP.NET Core
- Figma
- TensorFlow
- SEO Optimization
- Financial Modeling

BAD:
- Asp Net
- Design Tool Usage
- AI Math Library

--------------------------------------------------

4. MID-LEVEL GRANULARITY
Skills should NOT be:
- too broad
- too microscopic

GOOD:
- Authentication
- User Research
- Risk Assessment
- Docker
- Data Analysis

TOO BROAD:
- Programming
- Business
- Design
- Marketing

TOO SMALL:
- If Statement
- Button Padding
- Variable Declaration
- Excel SUM Formula

--------------------------------------------------

5. DOMAIN-AGNOSTIC
The system supports many industries.

Examples of GOOD skills by domain:

SOFTWARE:
- ASP.NET Core
- SignalR
- Docker
- REST API Design
- Microservices

DESIGN:
- UX Research
- Wireframing
- Visual Hierarchy
- Prototyping
- Typography

DATA:
- Machine Learning
- Data Cleaning
- Statistical Analysis
- Data Visualization

BUSINESS:
- Financial Modeling
- Market Research
- Business Strategy
- Project Planning

MARKETING:
- SEO Optimization
- Content Marketing
- Brand Strategy
- Campaign Analysis

CYBERSECURITY:
- Cryptography
- Threat Modeling
- Network Security
- Identity Management

--------------------------------------------------

6. NO CHALLENGE-SPECIFIC WORDING

NEVER generate:
- challenge names
- challenge descriptions
- scenario-specific labels
- explanatory phrases

BAD:
- Mathematical Understanding (Factorial)
- Chat App Logic
- Authentication Screen Building
- Login Security Challenge

GOOD:
- Recursion
- Authentication
- Secure Authentication
- Web Security

--------------------------------------------------

7. NO SENTENCES OR EXPLANATIONS

Skills must be SHORT competency names.

BAD:
- Persistence Mechanisms (File I/O, Database, ORM)
- Language/Framework Specifics (e.g., C#/.NET)

GOOD:
- File I/O
- Database Design
- Entity Framework Core
- C#

--------------------------------------------------

8. NO SOFT/ABSTRACT LABELS

Avoid vague non-measurable concepts.

BAD:
- Good Communication
- Smart Thinking
- Creativity
- Team Spirit

GOOD:
- Technical Writing
- Public Speaking
- Stakeholder Communication
- Presentation Design

==================================================
SKILL NORMALIZATION RULES
==================================================

Before creating a new skill:

STEP 1 — Exact Match
Search existing canonical skills.

STEP 2 — Alias Match
Search SkillAlias table.

STEP 3 — Fuzzy Match
Use similarity matching.

STEP 4 — Pending Review
If uncertain:
- create PendingSkill
- DO NOT auto-create canonical skill immediately

==================================================
FORMAT RULES
==================================================

Skill names should:
- use spaces properly
- avoid PascalCase concatenation
- avoid symbols unless official
- avoid parentheses unless industry standard
- use title casing

GOOD:
- Security Engineering
- Data Analysis
- ASP.NET Core
- UX Research

BAD:
- SecurityEngineering
- dataanalysis
- DataAnalysisSkill
- Analysis (Advanced)

